%[text] # Week 4 Lecture Demonstration: Parameter Sweeps with Hooke's Law
%[text] **LECTURE DEMONSTRATION**
%[text] PHY4605 Physics-First Reproducible MATLAB Notebook
%%
%[text] ## Physical Question and Learning Outcomes
%[text] A spring follows Hooke's law, $x=F/k$. If the same set of forces is applied to springs with different stiffness values, how can one controlled MATLAB sweep show the effect of stiffness without changing several things at once?
%[text] **Learning outcomes.** By the end of this demonstration, you can design a one-parameter sweep while holding the model and other inputs fixed, evaluate the same model for several parameter values and overlay labelled curves, and validate and explain the trend using a known or limiting case.
%%
%[text] ## Model, Assumptions, Variables, and Units
%[text] Use an ideal linear spring in its elastic range. Hooke's law is $F=kx$, so the extension is $x=F/k$. The applied force `F_N` is the common input array. Spring stiffness `k_Npm` is the parameter we deliberately vary. Extension `extension_m` is the output. All other assumptions remain unchanged between cases.
F_N = 0:0.5:10;             % applied force array (N)
k_Npm = [50 100 200];       % spring stiffness values (N/m)
baseline_k_Npm = 100;        % baseline spring stiffness (N/m)
%%
%[text] ## Predict Before Computing
%[text] For a fixed force, $x=F/k$ says that a larger stiffness must give a smaller extension. Therefore the 50 N/m spring should have the steepest force-extension curve, the 100 N/m case is the baseline, and the 200 N/m spring should have the smallest extension. At zero applied force, every ideal spring must have zero extension.
%%
%[text] ## Algorithm Before MATLAB
%[text] The controlled sweep has one rule: change only the parameter named in the question. Use this recipe: (1) define one common force array, (2) define a small justified stiffness array, (3) keep the same Hooke model for every case, (4) evaluate extension for each stiffness, (5) overlay all cases on the same labelled axes, (6) check the zero-force limiting case, and (7) explain the trend using $x=F/k$.
%%
%[text] ## Build the One-Parameter Sweep
%[text] Preallocate one row of `extension_m` for each stiffness value. The loop changes only `k_Npm(case_id)`. The force array and equation stay fixed, so any difference between rows can be attributed to stiffness.
extension_m = zeros(length(k_Npm),length(F_N));
for case_id = 1:length(k_Npm)
    extension_m(case_id,:) = F_N./k_Npm(case_id);
end
extension_m(:,1:5) %[output:662d2af9]
%%
%[text] ## Trace One Case and Identify the Baseline
%[text] Row 1 belongs to 50 N/m, row 2 to the 100 N/m baseline, and row 3 to 200 N/m. At 10 N, the baseline spring should extend $10/100=0.10$ m. Read the final column to compare all three springs at the same force.
baseline_extension_at_10N_m = extension_m(2,end) %[output:6c0aabd7]
extension_at_10N_m = extension_m(:,end) %[output:783723ce]
comparison_at_10N = table(k_Npm.',extension_at_10N_m, ... %[output:group:053a1795] %[output:78244656]
    'VariableNames',{'Stiffness_N_per_m','Extension_at_10N_m'}) %[output:group:053a1795] %[output:78244656]
%%
%[text] ## Overlay the Curves on One Set of Axes
%[text] A controlled comparison is easiest to read when every case uses the same axes. The transpose in `extension_m.'` changes the stored 3-by-21 result into a 21-by-3 plotting arrangement; it does not change the physics or the numerical values.
plot(F_N,extension_m.','LineWidth',2) %[output:6e0c45e3]
grid on %[output:6e0c45e3]
xlabel('Applied force, F (N)') %[output:6e0c45e3]
ylabel('Spring extension, x (m)') %[output:6e0c45e3]
title('Hooke''s Law Parameter Sweep: Effect of Spring Stiffness') %[output:6e0c45e3]
legend('k = 50 N/m','k = 100 N/m','k = 200 N/m','Location','northwest') %[output:6e0c45e3]
%%
%[text] ## Core Validation: Zero-Force Limiting Case
%[text] When $F=0$, Hooke's law gives $x=0/k=0$ for every positive stiffness. This limiting case is known before running MATLAB and tests every swept case at once.
zero_force_extension_m = extension_m(:,1) %[output:4a7f670e]
assert(max(abs(zero_force_extension_m)) < 1e-12, ...
    'Zero-force limiting-case validation failed.')
%%
%[text] ## Interpret the Trend Physically
%[text] All three curves are straight because the ideal Hooke model is linear in force. Increasing stiffness reduces the slope because the same applied force produces less extension. At 10 N, the 50 N/m, 100 N/m, and 200 N/m springs extend 0.20 m, 0.10 m, and 0.05 m respectively. The graph therefore supports the prediction that a stiffer spring deforms less under the same load.
%%
%[text] ## Diagnose a Plausible but Physically Wrong Calculation
%[text] Suppose an AI-generated expression uses multiplication, `x = F*k`, instead of division, `x = F/k`. MATLAB can evaluate the expression, but the units become N times N/m rather than metres, and the trend reverses: a stiffer spring would appear to extend more. A runnable expression is not enough; check the equation, units, and expected trend.
wrong_baseline_extension = F_N.*baseline_k_Npm;
correct_baseline_extension = F_N./baseline_k_Npm;
wrong_final_value = wrong_baseline_extension(end) %[output:8aba0274]
correct_final_value_m = correct_baseline_extension(end) %[output:942726a8]
%%
%[text] ## Working Exposure: A Two-Parameter Result Table
%[text] A table can compare selected forces and stiffnesses without introducing a new Core method. Here the supplied values show how the same physical relationship can be inspected numerically as well as graphically.
selected_force_N = [2 5 8];
selected_extension_m = selected_force_N./k_Npm.';
selected_extension_m %[output:2401dba0]
%%
%[text] ## Optional Stretch: Quantitative Local Sensitivity
%[text] This is not required for the Core Week 4 route. At fixed force, the local sensitivity of extension to stiffness is $\\partial x/\\partial k=-F/k^2$. The negative sign formalises the trend already visible in the graph. Quantitative sensitivity becomes a main topic later in the course.
dx_dk_m2_per_N = -F_N./baseline_k_Npm^2;
%%
%[text] ## Three Takeaways
%[text] First, a parameter sweep is a controlled computational experiment: deliberately vary one parameter and keep the model and other inputs fixed. Second, overlay curves on common labelled axes so each case can be compared at the same physical input. Third, accept the trend only after it agrees with the equation, units, and a known or limiting case.
%%
%[text] ## Reproducibility Record
%[text] Run sections from top to bottom in a fresh MATLAB session. This demonstration requires MATLAB R2025a or later and base MATLAB only. Locked values are `F_N=0:0.5:10`, `k_Npm=[50 100 200]` N/m, and baseline stiffness `100` N/m. No hidden Workspace state is required.

